The partition logic is correct, but the previous averaging expression can underflow for very small numbers. These are manual traces, not executed tests:

- Empty array: `[], [7]`. Cuts `(0,1)` give boundaries `(-Infinity, Infinity)` and `(7, Infinity)`. Both cross-comparisons pass; the odd-length result is `7`.
- Disjoint arrays: `[1,2], [3,4]`. Cuts `(1,1)` fail because `3 > 2`, so `low` becomes `2`. Cuts `(2,0)` pass; the middle values are `2` and `3`, returning `2.5`.
- Duplicates: `[2], [2]`. Cuts `(0,1)` pass, including equality; the result is `2`.
- Falsifying case: `[Number.MIN_VALUE], [Number.MIN_VALUE]`. Cuts `(0,1)` pass, but the preceding code divides each middle value by two, rounding both to zero. Adding before dividing correctly returns `Number.MIN_VALUE`. If addition overflows, dividing first remains the fallback.

The search maintains a feasible partition within `[low, high]`, with exactly `floor((m+n+1)/2)` elements on the left. Searching the shorter array keeps the other cut within bounds.

```javascript
function solve(nums1, nums2) {
  if (nums1.length > nums2.length) {
    [nums1, nums2] = [nums2, nums1];
  }

  const m = nums1.length;
  const n = nums2.length;
  const half = Math.floor((m + n + 1) / 2);
  let low = 0;
  let high = m;

  while (low <= high) {
    const i = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);
    const j = half - i;

    const left1 = i === 0 ? -Infinity : nums1[i - 1];
    const right1 = i === m ? Infinity : nums1[i];
    const left2 = j === 0 ? -Infinity : nums2[j - 1];
    const right2 = j === n ? Infinity : nums2[j];

    if (left1 <= right2 && left2 <= right1) {
      const leftMax = Math.max(left1, left2);
      if ((m + n) % 2 === 1) return leftMax;

      const rightMin = Math.min(right1, right2);
      const sum = leftMax + rightMin;
      return Number.isFinite(sum)
        ? sum / 2
        : leftMax / 2 + rightMin / 2;
    }

    if (left1 > right2) {
      high = i - 1;
    } else {
      low = i + 1;
    }
  }
}
```

